tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:15:23: error[unwritable_name]: this library's name holds `>`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `a>b` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `link "m"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:18:23: error[unwritable_name]: this library's name holds `"`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `a\"b` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `link "m"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:21:23: error[unwritable_name]: this library's name holds `\`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `a\\b` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `link "m"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:24:23: error[unwritable_name]: this library's name holds `/`, which every linker reads as a directory and not the same one, `sub/m.lib` on Windows and `libsub/m` on macOS and Linux, so `sub/m` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `link "m"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:27:26: error[unwritable_name]: this package's name holds `>`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `p>q` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `package "raylib"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:30:26: error[unwritable_name]: this package's name holds `"`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `p\"q` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `package "raylib"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:33:26: error[unwritable_name]: this package's name holds `\`, which no file's name on Windows can hold, so `p\\q` does not name one file on every machine: write the name without it, as `package "raylib"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:36:26: error[package_comparison]: this package's name holds `=`, which is how `pkg-config` writes a version's comparison, and a group names a package and no version: write the package's name alone, as `package "raylib"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:39:26: error[package_comparison]: this package's name holds `!`, which is how `pkg-config` writes a version's comparison, and a group names a package and no version: write the package's name alone, as `package "raylib"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:42:23: error[machine_locked_path]: `C:\\side\\m` names a path, and a library in a group head says what the machine has, not where this machine keeps it — the next machine keeps it elsewhere
  pass `--library <dir>` with the directory holding it, or name a `package` and let `pkg-config` answer; `link` takes the library's **name**, as `link "sqlite3"`
tests/golden/check/fixedbugs-252-a-library-name-no-windows-linker-reads-as-one-file.hero:45:8: error[undefined_header_name]: this header's name holds `"`, which C leaves undefined in a header's name (C11 6.4.7): one compiler reads it as itself and the next need not, so `a\"b.h` means what one compiler says
  rename the header without it
